          ],
          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 104,
                "end_index": 183,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Historically, humans suffered\u2014and frequently died\u2014from waterborne illnesses"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 187,
                "end_index": 305,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "However, before the invention of modern water treatment and purification (like chlorination and industrial filtration)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 389,
                "end_index": 509,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Low Population Density and Nomadic Lifestyles\nFor roughly 95% of human history, humans lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 512,
                "end_index": 702,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Lack of Human Contamination:** Waterborne pathogens like cholera, typhoid, and dysentery are primarily spread through fecal-oral contamination when human waste gets into the water supply"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 704,
                "end_index": 838,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because nomadic bands were small and constantly moving, they rarely stayed in one place long enough to pollute their own water sources"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 841,
                "end_index": 994,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Pristine Natural Sources:** Early humans relied on naturally clean, moving water\u2014such as fast-flowing rivers, mountain streams, and natural springs"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 996,
                "end_index": 1136,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "They also relied on deep groundwater, which is naturally filtered of pathogens by layers of soil, sand, and stone as it trickles underground"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1146,
                "end_index": 1268,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Senses and Evolutionary Instincts\nLike other animals, humans evolved sharp sensory instincts to evaluate their environment"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1270,
                "end_index": 1397,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The \"Sniff and Taste\" Test:** Our ancestors relied heavily on their senses of sight, smell, and taste to judge water safety"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1399,
                "end_index": 1528,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "They avoided stagnant, murky, or foul-smelling water because evolutionary adaptation associated these traits with rot and illness"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1914,
                "end_index": 1985,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To adapt, civilizations turned to drinks that were naturally sterilized"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2126,
                "end_index": 2294,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Brewing beer requires boiling the water (which kills bacteria), and the subsequent fermentation produces alcohol and acidity, both of which act as natural disinfectants"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2296,
                "end_index": 2437,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In medieval Europe, weak \"small beer\" (low-alcohol beer) was widely consumed by adults and children alike as a safe alternative to city water"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2439,
                "end_index": 2576,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Boiled Beverages (Tea):** In ancient East Asia, particularly China, the custom of drinking boiled water infused with tea leaves arose"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2578,
                "end_index": 2723,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Though they did not understand germ theory, they recognized that boiling water prevented the \"belly aches\" and sickness associated with raw water"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                15
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2733,
                "end_index": 2896,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Rudimentary \"Proto-Purification\"\nWhile *modern* purification was not yet invented, humans have been experimenting with basic water treatment for thousands of years"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2733,
                "end_index": 2997,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Rudimentary \"Proto-Purification\"\nWhile *modern* purification was not yet invented, humans have been experimenting with basic water treatment for thousands of years:\n* **Decantation:** Simply letting muddy water sit in a vessel so the sediment settled to the bottom"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                16,
                17
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2999,
                "end_index": 3144,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Natural Filtration:** Ancient Sanskrit and Egyptian texts dating back 4,000 years describe filtering water through sand, gravel, and charcoal"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                16,
                17
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3147,
                "end_index": 3291,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Flocculation:** Ancient Egyptians used minerals like alum to bind to suspended dirt in the water, causing it to clump and sink to the bottom"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                18
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3301,
                "end_index": 3494,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Biological Adaptation and a Grim Reality\nFinally, it is important to note that survival was a numbers game:\n* **Localized Immunity:** A person\u2019s gut microbiome adapts to the local environment"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                18
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3496,
                "end_index": 3679,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "People who lived in a specific area developed a baseline tolerance to the ambient bacteria in their local water source\u2014bacteria that would make an outsider or traveler violently ill"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3754,
                "end_index": 3911,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Before water sanitation, waterborne parasites and bacteria were among the leading causes of death in human history, especially for infants and young children"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3913,
                "end_index": 4052,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Humanity survived not because everyone drank water safely, but because high birth rates offset the immense toll taken by contaminated water"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
          "retrieval_metadata": null,
